course: everyday
lesson: 16
title: Finding your way with landmarks
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A landmark is something easy to see and remember, like a tall tower, a statue, a bridge, a river or a big red door.
explain: When you go somewhere new, pick a landmark at each turn, and look back now and then, because the way back looks different from the way there.
explain: Streets have names on signs, and houses have numbers; usually odd numbers are on one side of the street and even numbers on the other.
explain: The Sun rises roughly in the east and sets roughly in the west; at midday it is in the south if you live in the northern half of the world (USA, Canada, UK, Ireland) and in the north if you live in the southern half (Australia, New Zealand).
explain: Example: Liam in Toronto remembers «left at the library, right at the bridge, our building is opposite the park» — and finds his way home.
explain: A boy in Yorkshire took a short cut through a wood without looking at where he was going; he got lost and wandered for hours before a search team found him cold and frightened.
explain: A girl in a big shopping centre in Sydney got separated from her dad; she remembered they had parked by the red lion statue and asked a shop worker to take her there.
fact: A landmark is a feature that is easy to recognise and remember.
fact: Good landmarks do not move: a tower, a church, a bridge or a statue — not a parked car or a dog.
fact: A route looks different on the way back, so looking behind you helps you recognise it later.
fact: Street names are written on signs, often at corners.
fact: On most streets, houses with odd numbers are on one side and even numbers on the other.
fact: On most maps, north is at the top.
fact: A compass needle points roughly north.
fact: The Sun rises roughly in the east and sets roughly in the west.
fact: At midday the Sun is in the southern part of the sky in the USA, Canada, the UK and Ireland.
fact: At midday the Sun is in the northern part of the sky in Australia and New Zealand.
fact: Moss can grow on any damp, shady side of a tree, so it is not a reliable way to find north.
fact: Phone maps are helpful, but batteries run out, so it is wise to know your route too.
fact: Children who have not noticed landmarks often walk the wrong way and get more lost.
fact: Remembering a big, fixed landmark helps you and the adults find each other again.
exercise: What is a landmark? | Something easy to see and remember | Any car parked along a street | A word printed on a map | The number of steps you have walked
exercise: Which is the best landmark for remembering a route? | A tall clock tower | A parked blue car | A cat sitting on a wall | A puddle on the path
exercise: Why should you sometimes look back on a new route? | The way back looks different | It makes the walk shorter | It keeps you walking straight | It helps you walk faster
exercise: Where do you usually find street names? | On signs, often at corners | Painted on every front door | On the roofs of buses | Only inside the shops
exercise: House number 14 is on one side of the street. Where is number 15 most likely to be? | On the opposite side | Next door on the same side | At the far end of town | In the next street along
exercise: In which direction does the Sun rise, roughly? | East | West | North | South
exercise: Where is the Sun at midday for a girl in Chicago? | In the southern part of the sky | In the northern part of the sky | Straight overhead every day | Low in the eastern sky
exercise: Where is the Sun at midday for a boy in Melbourne? | In the northern part of the sky | In the southern part of the sky | Straight overhead every day | Low in the western sky
exercise: On most maps, which direction is at the top? | North | South | East | West
exercise: Can moss on tree trunks reliably show you north? | No, it grows on whichever side is damp and shady | Yes, moss grows only on the north side of a tree trunk | Yes, but only on big old trees | No, moss always grows on the south side
exercise: Why is it wise to know your route even if you have a phone map? | The battery could run out | Phone maps never show real streets | Phones do not work outdoors | Maps on phones are always wrong
exercise: Which directions would help a friend find your house best? | Left at the library, our door is opposite the park | Near some cars and a few trees, not far from a corner | Walk for a while, then turn somewhere | The house with a cat in the window
exercise: What does a compass needle point to? | Roughly north | Always to your home | The nearest city | Towards the Sun
exercise: You are walking towards the setting Sun. Which way are you facing, roughly? | West | East | North | South
test: Zoe walks to a new friend's house and notes «left at the bakery, right at the bridge». Why is this useful? | Fixed places help her find the way back | Bakeries always face north, so they work like a compass | It makes the walk much shorter | Bridges are always in the town centre
test: Which would be a poor landmark? | A food truck parked on the corner | The town hall clock above the square | A big stone statue | The old railway bridge over the river
test: A boy in Wellington wants to use the midday Sun to find north. Where should he look? | Towards the Sun | Away from the Sun | To the left of the Sun | Straight up
test: A girl in Dublin faces the midday Sun. Which way is she facing? | South | North | East | West
test: Which is true about walking a route for the first time? | Looking back helps you recognise it on the way home | The way back always looks exactly the same as the way out | Landmarks are only useful at night | You should never look behind you
test: You are at number 22 and need number 28 in the same street. Where should you look? | On the same side, a few houses along | Straight across the street on the other side | In the next street over | Back at the first corner
test: Early in the morning the Sun is on your right. Which way are you facing, roughly? | North | South | East | West
test: Your phone map stops working in a town you have walked through. What helps most? | Remembering the landmarks you passed | Shaking the phone very hard | Looking for moss on the trees | Walking straight on in any direction
test: Why is a river a good landmark? | It is big, fixed and easy to recognise | It moves to a new place every day | It always flows towards your house | It looks the same in every town
test: walking to nan's new place, what do i remember the way by | Things that don't move, like a church | Parked cars along the road, they're easy to spot | The dog in a garden | Where the clouds are
test: why'd you keep looking back on the walk | The way back looks different | To check nobody follows | Because it is quicker | To count the houses
test: does moss grow on the north side? | Not reliably, it grows where it's damp and shady | Yes, always on the north side, that's how you find north | Only on the south side | Yes, moss is a compass
test: sun's going down, which way's that | Roughly west | Roughly east | Roughly north | Straight south
test: we're in a new park, how do i not get lost | Notice big landmarks and look back at them often | Follow any path that looks nice and you'll end up back somewhere | Only look at your feet | Just run until you see the car
test: lost dad at the mall, we were near the fountain | Stay or go back to the fountain and ask a worker | Leave the mall and wait by the car until he comes out | Walk around until you see him | Go with anyone who offers a lift
test: Leo says looking back at the way u came is pointless. true? | No, the way back looks different from the way out | Yes, roads look the same both ways | Yes, you only need to look ahead | Yes, unless it is dark
test: did u hear a family got lost hiking cos they followed the moss to find north and ended up in the wrong state | Real lesson: moss is not a reliable compass; «wrong state» is likely blown up | Real lesson: moss always grows on the north side, so they must have read it wrong | It proves families should never go hiking | It means only maps from shops can be trusted
test: my brother says the sun's always straight above you at noon everywhere so it can't help you find your way | Not true; at midday the Sun is in the south of the sky in the UK and USA, and in the north in Australia | He is right, it is overhead everywhere at noon | He is right, the Sun moves randomly each day | He is right, so only phones can show direction
test: Aiden got lost on the school trip cos he's thick, that's what Jake says, pass it on | Do not pass it on; getting lost can happen to anyone and calling him thick is cruel | Pass it on, since getting lost on a trip proves he never listens to anything | Pass it on, so he pays attention next time | Pass it on, but only to the teachers
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a suitable landmark for the purpose of finding one's way? | A feature that is readily recognisable and does not move, such as a tower or a bridge | A parked vehicle of a distinctive colour | A dog that is seen upon a particular street | A cloud of unusual shape above the route
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person ought to look behind him whilst walking a new route. | A route presents a different appearance upon the return journey | It enables him to walk the route at greater speed on the following occasion | It prevents others from following the route | It is required by the rules of the road
exam: It being given that the observer is situated in Australia at midday, select the part of the sky in which the Sun appears. | The northern part of the sky | The southern part of the sky | Directly beneath the horizon | The western part of the sky
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the use of moss for the determination of north. | Moss may grow upon any damp and shady side of a tree and is therefore unreliable | Moss grows exclusively upon the northern side of trees | Moss grows exclusively upon the southern side of trees | Moss points towards north in the manner of a compass needle
